

================================================================================
Benchmark: torch.cat vs direct copy for MLA k_nope/k_pe concatenation
================================================================================
Tensor shapes: k_nope=[B, 128, 128], k_pe=[B, 1, 64]
dtype: bfloat16

  Batch Size |   cat (ms) |  direct (ms) |  Speedup |  Reduction
----------------------------------------------------------------------
          32 |      0.124 |        0.229 |    0.54x |     -85.0%
          64 |      0.252 |        0.215 |    1.17x |      14.5%
         128 |      0.321 |        0.244 |    1.31x |      23.9%
         256 |      0.374 |        0.296 |    1.27x |      21.1%
         512 |      0.728 |        0.561 |    1.30x |      23.0%
        1024 |      1.436 |        1.110 |    1.29x |      22.7%
        2048 |      2.851 |        2.201 |    1.30x |      22.8%
        4096 |      5.565 |        4.560 |    1.22x |      18.1%
        8192 |     11.153 |        9.063 |    1.23x |      18.7%
       16384 |     22.219 |       18.108 |    1.23x |      18.5%
       32768 |     44.450 |       36.171 |    1.23x |      18.6%
       65536 |     88.854 |       72.301 |    1.23x |      18.6%
================================================================================

Speedup summary:
  Min:  0.54x
  Max:  1.31x
  Mean: 1.19x

Conclusion:
  - Direct copy becomes beneficial at batch size >= 64
  - For batch sizes >= 512: avg speedup = 1.25x
  - MLA prefill typically uses large batches, so optimization is effective


